input: goodix: don't wait out the buffer-status timeout while idle

The GT9271 on the reTerminal DM scans at 100 Hz (its config sets a
10 ms coordinate refresh period), yet the delivered report rate maxes
out at ~43-45 Hz.

After a 'finger up' event the panel sends a few spurious interrupts in
which the buffer status bit never gets set (see the comment introduced
by upstream commit 9b5db7aab4d6). Each of those keeps the threaded IRQ
handler inside the GOODIX_BUFFER_STATUS_TIMEOUT poll loop for the full
20 ms. Interrupts arriving during that window collapse into a single
rerun of the thread (IRQTF_RUNTHREAD is a single bit), so real scan
cycles are skipped and the effective report period roughly doubles.

Bail out of the poll loop early when no contacts are active. Nothing
can be lost in that state: while a finger is on the panel the
controller keeps scanning, so the next interrupt arrives within one
scan period, and the buffer status bit latches until the host clears
0x814E - a 'finger down' whose status bit lags its interrupt is picked
up by the following interrupt. 'Finger up' always happens while
contacts are active and therefore still gets the full 20 ms timeout,
preserving the correctness fix from commit 9b5db7aab4d6.

This is the IRQ-path counterpart of the polling-path fix in
raspberrypi/linux commit 42bcead3ece6 ("input: goodix: Don't retry
individual reads when polling").

--- a/drivers/input/touchscreen/goodix.h	2026-08-02 15:56:15
+++ b/drivers/input/touchscreen/goodix.h	2026-08-02 15:56:20
@@ -93,6 +93,7 @@
 	u16 version;
 	bool reset_controller_at_probe;
 	bool load_cfg_from_disk;
+	bool contacts_active;
 	int pen_input_registered;
 	struct completion firmware_loading_complete;
 	unsigned long irq_flags;
--- a/drivers/input/touchscreen/goodix.c	2026-08-02 15:56:15
+++ b/drivers/input/touchscreen/goodix.c	2026-08-02 15:56:29
@@ -305,6 +305,21 @@
 			 * versions continuously report "not ready" if there are
 			 * no touch points.
 			 */
+			break;
+
+		/*
+		 * After 'finger up' the panel sends a few spurious interrupts
+		 * with the buffer status bit never set; waiting out the full
+		 * timeout on each of those stalls the IRQ thread for 20 ms
+		 * and roughly halves the effective report rate. Nothing is
+		 * lost by bailing out while no contacts are active: the
+		 * controller keeps scanning while a finger is on the panel
+		 * (the next interrupt arrives within one scan period) and the
+		 * buffer status bit latches until cleared, so a delayed
+		 * 'finger down' is picked up on the next interrupt. Releases
+		 * happen while contacts are active and get the full timeout.
+		 */
+		if (!ts->contacts_active)
 			break;
 
 		usleep_range(1000, 2000); /* Poll every 1 - 2 ms */
@@ -483,6 +498,9 @@
 	if (touch_num < 0)
 		return;
 
+	ts->contacts_active = touch_num > 0 ||
+			      (point_data[0] & GOODIX_HAVE_KEY);
+
 	/* The pen being down is always reported as a single touch */
 	if (touch_num == 1 && (point_data[1] & 0x80)) {
 		goodix_ts_report_pen_down(ts, point_data);
